{"type":"route","meta":{"status":200,"headers":{"cache-control":"public, max-age=3600","content-type":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8","x-robots-tag":"noindex","x-next-cache-tags":"_N_T_/layout,_N_T_/md/layout,_N_T_/md/[...slug]/layout,_N_T_/md/[...slug]/route,_N_T_/md/blog/choosing-a-wedding-invitation-theme"}},"body":"# Choosing a wedding invitation theme that still looks right in six months\n\n> How to pick a visual style for your invitation: what to match it to, what to ignore, and the tests worth running before you commit.\n\n- Source: http://localhost:3000/blog/choosing-a-wedding-invitation-theme\n- Author: Prachi Jain, Founder, Amantrika\n- Published: 28 July 2026\n- Category: Inspiration\n- Tags: Themes, Design, Invitation\n- Reading time: 4 min\n\n---\nMost theme advice is about taste, which is not much help when two families have four opinions. What follows is about the decisions that turn out to matter afterwards.\n\n## Match the tradition before the aesthetic\n\nThe first filter is not \"what do I like\". It is \"what does this wedding actually look like\". A Nikah invitation and a Punjabi wedding invitation are not interchangeable with a colour swap — the motifs, the scripts and the greeting lines are different, and guests notice immediately when they are wrong.\n\nStart by narrowing to the themes built for your tradition, then choose within that set. It cuts a paralysing decision down to three or four options, and it removes the risk of the mistake that actually embarrasses people.\n\n## Match the formality of the function\n\nThe other useful filter is how formal the invitation should feel — and this varies by function, not by wedding.\n\n- **The wedding itself** is the most formal thing you will send. This is where ornate, traditional and restrained all work.\n- **Sangeet and mehndi** invitations can be much lighter. A playful theme that would be wrong for the wedding is right here.\n- **The reception** sits somewhere between, and often goes to a wider list including colleagues, which pushes it back towards formal.\n\nIf you are sending one invitation covering everything, it should sit at the formality of the most formal function on it.\n\n## Four tests worth running before you commit\n\n### Look at it on a phone, at arm's length\nNot on your laptop. Almost every guest will open it on a phone, many outdoors, several with the brightness down. If the names are not immediately readable at arm's length, the theme is too delicate.\n### Screenshot it and send it to yourself\nGuests screenshot invitations constantly to forward them. Some themes survive that and some turn into a grey smear. Test it.\n### Put the longest name on it\nCompound surnames, both sets of parents, a long venue name. Ornate frames that look beautiful with \"Ananya & Rohan\" often break down with the full formal names.\n### Show it to someone from the older generation\nNot for approval — for legibility. If your grandmother has to hold the phone at three distances to read the date, the type is too small or the contrast too low, and she is exactly the guest you cannot afford to lose.\n\n### Try more than one\nBecause content and theme are stored separately, switching themes costs nothing and loses nothing. Build the invitation once, then look at it in three or four themes before deciding. Most people change their mind after seeing their own names in it, not before.\n\n## What matters less than people think\n\n**Matching the decor.** Guests receive the invitation months before they see the mandap, and almost never compare them. Spend the energy on legibility instead.\n\n**The trend.** Invitations are looked at for a few weeks and then, occasionally, for decades. A theme chosen because it is current tends to date exactly as fast as the trend that produced it.\n\n**Animation.** A nice opening is a nice opening. It is not what makes an invitation good, and on a poor connection it is the first thing to become an obstacle. If a theme's animation delays the names appearing, that is a defect, not a feature.\n\n## What matters more than people think\n\n**Contrast.** The single most common problem: gold text on cream looks exquisite on a designer's monitor and disappears on a phone in daylight.\n\n**How the schedule reads.** Most guests come to the invitation for one piece of information — when and where. A theme that makes the schedule beautiful but hard to scan is working against the thing the invitation is for.\n\n**The script your names are set in.** If any part of the invitation is in Hindi, Tamil, Gujarati or Urdu, the theme needs a typeface that renders that script properly. A theme that falls back to a system font for the Devanagari line will look visibly unfinished next to its English.\n\n## A reasonable way to decide\n\nNarrow by tradition. Narrow again by formality. Put your real names and your real venue into the two or three that remain. Look at all of them on a phone, one after another, and pick the one where you can read everything without trying.\n\nThat is usually the right answer, and it is rarely the one that looked best in the theme gallery.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Can I change the theme after I've built the invitation?\n\nIn Amantrika, yes — the content and the visual theme are stored separately, so switching one does not touch the other. Nothing you have written is lost when you change how it looks.\n\n### Should the invitation match the wedding decor?\n\nLoosely. Guests see the invitation months before the decor and rarely hold the two side by side. Matching the tradition and the formality matters far more than matching a colour.\n\n### What colours should I avoid on a wedding invitation?\n\nAnything that fails to hold up in a screenshot on a dim phone screen — very dark palettes and low-contrast pastels both suffer. Regional associations matter too: white alone reads as mourning in several Indian traditions.\n"}